      <div id="rNeeds"></div>
      <div id="rConcerns"></div>
      <div id="rBenchmark"></div>
      <div id="rSequence"></div>
      <div id="rWatch"></div>
      <div id="rTests"></div>
      <div id="rPrograms"></div>
      <div id="rFind"></div>
      <div id="rCompare"></div>
      <div id="rSystems"></div>
      <div id="rNumbers"></div>
      <div id="rResources"></div>

      <div class="card card-pad audit-cta">
        <h3>Estimates end where the audit begins</h3>
        <p>
          Everything above is based on how homes like yours typically perform (scaled to your bills if
          you entered them), not a model of your actual house. A home energy audit, with a blower door
          test, a duct leakage test where the ducts run outside the living space, an infrared scan,
          combustion safety checks and, for new equipment, a Manual J load calculation, turns these
          ranges into numbers for <em>your</em> address. The federal $150 audit tax credit ended with
          2025, but utility and state programs commonly pay for most or all of an assessment.
        </p>
        <p class="cta-links"><a href="/us/find/">Our guide to finding an auditor →</a> <a href="/us/how-it-works/">How an audit works →</a> <a href="/us/cost/">What an audit costs →</a></p>
      </div>

      <LeadFormUS heading="Want a person to look at your plan?" sub={leadSub} context="us-planner" />

      <p class="small disclaimer">
        Estimates are ranges based on how similar homes perform: heating from building-science
        starting points for each era, cooling from EIA’s 2020 survey of US homes, your area’s
        climate (NOAA) and your state’s energy prices (EIA), with the assumptions
        <a href="#assumptions">listed below</a>. They are deliberately conservative and are not a
        guarantee of savings. Program details change, so confirm on official pages before spending.
        Nothing you enter here is stored or sent anywhere: the planner runs entirely in your browser,
        and this page loads no analytics. Your answers sit only in this page’s address, so a link you
        copy carries them.
      </p>
      <div class="wizard-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" id="restartBtn">Start over</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" id="copyLink">Copy link to this plan</button>
      </div>

      <div class="sticky-book" id="stickyBook">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" id="stickyBookBtn" href="/us/find/">Book my audit</a>
        <a class="sb-next" href="#rNext">My next 3 steps</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="assumptions" id="assumptions">
      <details id="assumptionsBox">
        <summary><h2>How the estimates work, and every assumption behind them</h2></summary>
      <p class="small measure">
        The first three digits of your ZIP code place the home in a state and, where we cover it, one
        of the 26 largest metro areas, using Census Bureau ZIP code areas, population and the 2023
        metro definitions. The metro sets the climate: heating and cooling degree days from NOAA’s
        1991–2020 normals at its main airport, and the climate profile that decides which problems,
        checks and wording fit. Outside those metros the planner uses the nearest covered metro in
        your state, or a stand-in you pick, and says so. Energy prices are your state’s averages from
        the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA). If you enter a gas, oil or propane bill, the
        heating estimate is scaled to it; if you enter a summer and a winter electric bill, they are
        split by degree days into air conditioning, heating and everything else, and the matching
        estimate is scaled to them (always within 0.5× to 1.6× of a typical house). An energy audit
        replaces all of this with measurements. Program and price data were checked on {US_VERIFIED};
        the cooling model on {payload.coolChecked}.
      </p>

      <div class="tablewrap card">
        <table class="stack era-table">
          <caption>A typical house of each era, as the heating model starts it</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Built</th><th>Heat needed</th><th>Attic</th><th>Walls</th><th>Basement walls</th><th>Windows</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {eraRows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Built">{r.label}</td>
                <td data-label="Heat needed">{r.intensity.toFixed(0)} kBtu per sq ft a year at {int(REF_HDD65)}&nbsp;°F degree days</td>
                <td data-label="Attic">R-{r.atticR}</td>
                <td data-label="Walls">R-{r.wallR}</td>
                <td data-label="Basement walls">R-{r.basementR}</td>
                <td data-label="Windows">{WINDOW_LABEL_US[r.window]}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small measure">
        These are typical starting points for a house of each age, not measurements: building-science
        archetypes for the insulation, windows and air leakage of each era, the same ones our
        calculator engine uses across the site. US homes of the same age vary widely, and an audit
        measures yours. Heat needed is scaled to your climate’s degree days. Townhouses, rowhouses and
        duplexes use 90% of the detached figure, and the four size bands are modeled as about
        {sizeSqft.map((s) => s.toLocaleString('en-US')).join(', ')} sq ft.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-2 same-diff">
        <div class="card card-pad">
          <h3>Heating: what the model assumes</h3>
          <ul class="small">
            <li>Heating need per square foot by era, house sizes and the starting insulation levels and windows above, scaled from NOAA 65&nbsp;°F degree days (divided by 1.8 for the engine’s Celsius degree days).</li>
            <li>Upgrade physics: the R-value, window U-factor and air-leakage formulas, multiplied by {MODEL.envelope.F.aboveGrade} above grade and {MODEL.envelope.F.basement} for basement walls; no single upgrade credited with more than {Math.round(MODEL.envelope.capSingle * 100)}% of heating, and all together with no more than {Math.round(MODEL.envelope.capTotal * 100)}%. Air sealing is credited with a {Math.round(MODEL.envelope.air.reductionDefault * 100)}% cut in leakage, which is taken as {Math.round(MODEL.envelope.air.leakShareOfHeatLoss * 100)}% of heat loss.</li>
            <li>Equipment efficiencies: gas furnaces 80% and 95%, gas boilers 82% and 89%, oil 60–85%, propane 82% and 93%, electric resistance 100%, geothermal heat pumps a seasonal COP of {(MODEL.systems.gshp as { eff: number }).eff}. Air-source heat pumps have a seasonal COP (backup heat included) that falls as winters get colder: cold-climate models {bands[0].coldClimate} below {int((bands[0].maxHdd ?? 0) * 1.8 + 1.8)}&nbsp;°F heating degree days, down to {bands[bands.length - 1].coldClimate} above {int(bands[bands.length - 1].minHdd * 1.8)}. Heat pump upgrades are priced as cold-climate models where winters reach 4,000 heating degree days, standard models elsewhere.</li>
            <li>Hot water: about {gallonsPerPerson} gallons per person a day for a household of three, heated to {deliveryF}&nbsp;°F, with the cold-water temperature worked out from the degree days. Heat pump water heaters are charged for the heat they take from the house only during the heating share of the year (heating degree days as a share of all degree days, at most two-thirds), so in hot climates that charge is small.</li>
            <li>Thermostat setbacks save about {Math.round(MODEL.setback.perDegPer8h * 100)}% of heating per degree Celsius (1.8&nbsp;°F) held for 8 hours; heat pumps limited to about 4&nbsp;°F.</li>
            <li>Attic target: ENERGY STAR’s retrofit figure for an uninsulated attic in your IECC climate zone: R-{insulation.attic['1']} in zone 1, R-{insulation.attic['2']} in zones 2–3 and R-{insulation.attic['4']} in zones 4–8.</li>
            <li>Units: therms (natural gas converted at EIA’s {PRICE_PERIODS.btuPerCf.toLocaleString('en-US')} Btu per cubic foot), gallons, kilowatt-hours and US dollars. Propane water heaters are given the model’s gas tank efficiency.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-pad">
          <h3>Cooling and bills: what the model assumes</h3>
          <ul class="small">
            <li>Air conditioning: EIA’s 2020 Residential Energy Consumption Survey (RECS) microdata for single-family detached homes with central AC, indoor fan included, one benchmark per metro (its state and climate cell), rescaled to the metro’s normal cooling degree days (exponent {CD.p.cddExp.A} in humid and dry zones, {CD.p.cddExp.C} in marine ones), the home’s size (exponent {CD.p.areaExp}), era (from ×{CD.p.era['pre-1946']} before 1946 to ×{CD.p.era['2011-plus']} since 2011) and type (townhouses ×{CD.p.type.semi}, condos ×{CD.p.type.condo5plus}). Window units use RECS figures per household by zone. The typical range runs from {CD.p.range.lo}× to {CD.p.range.hi}× ({CD.p.range.mlo}× to {CD.p.range.mhi}× in marine climates).</li>
            <li>AC efficiency by age, from federal standards and LBNL shipment data: a central AC under 10 years old about SEER {CD.seer.centralNewS} in the South (where federal minimums are higher) and {CD.seer.centralNewN} elsewhere; 10 years or more, about SEER {CD.seer.centralOldNew} in houses built since 1996 and {CD.seer.centralOldOld} in older ones; heat pumps about SEER {CD.seer.hpNew} under 15 years and {CD.seer.hpOld} over. A new variable-speed unit is about SEER {CD.seer.newVs} ({CD.seer.newVs2} SEER2).</li>
            <li>Cooling savings, as a share of the AC estimate (typical, from NREL ResStock and Florida Solar Energy Center field studies): duct sealing {CD.m.duct[1]}% where ducts run through an attic or crawlspace; a radiant barrier {CD.m.radiant[1]}% over thin insulation; a cool roof {CD.m.coolRoof[1]}%; window film or solar screens {CD.m.film[1]}% and low-SHGC windows {CD.m.lowShgc[1]}% on clear glass; air sealing up to {CD.m.air[1]}–5% by zone; attic insulation by a formula on the before and after R-values; raising the cooling setting by the metro’s share per degree. Measures combine without double counting: attic insulation, a radiant barrier and a cool roof overlap, so the largest counts in full and the others at half.</li>
            <li>Electric bills: a two-bill degree-day split, a two-point version of the billing analysis in DOE’s Uniform Methods Project, using NOAA’s monthly normals. It falls back to the typical house, and says why, when the months’ weather is too similar, the summer bill isn’t higher, or the non-heating, non-cooling use comes out outside 5–60 kWh a day.</li>
            <li>Prices: EIA state averages, shown with the month or week they cover, and U.S. averages, labeled national, where EIA has no state figure. Savings are valued at these averages, which include fixed charges, so they can run a little high.</li>
            <li>Left out: crawlspace savings, installed costs and paybacks (we don’t have reliable US prices for them yet), and pools, EVs and other large loads.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <details class="card data-details">
        <summary><strong>Energy prices by state</strong> <span class="small">(EIA, as used by the planner)</span></summary>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table class="stack">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>State</th>
                <th>Electricity, {PRICE_PERIODS.elecMonth}</th>
                <th>Natural gas, {PRICE_PERIODS.gasYear} average</th>
                <th>Heating oil, week of {PRICE_PERIODS.oilWeek}</th>
                <th>Propane, week of {PRICE_PERIODS.propaneWeek}</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {priceRows.map((s) => (
                <tr>
                  <td data-label="State">{s.n}</td>
                  <td data-label={`Electricity, ${PRICE_PERIODS.elecMonth}`}>{s.p.e.toFixed(1)}¢/kWh</td>
                  <td data-label={`Natural gas, ${PRICE_PERIODS.gasYear} average`}>{s.p.g !== null ? `${usd(s.p.g)}/therm${s.p.gy ? ` (${s.p.gy} average; no ${PRICE_PERIODS.gasYear} figure)` : ''}` : `U.S. ${usd(NATIONAL.gasPerTherm)}/therm used`}</td>
                  <td data-label="Heating oil">{s.p.o !== null ? `${usd(s.p.o)}/gal` : `none published; U.S. ${usd(NATIONAL.oilPerGal)} used`}</td>
                  <td data-label="Propane">{s.p.pr !== null ? `${usd(s.p.pr)}/gal` : `none published; U.S. ${usd(NATIONAL.propanePerGal)} used`}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              <tr class="us-row">
                <td data-label="State">United States (national)</td>
                <td data-label={`Electricity, ${PRICE_PERIODS.elecMonth}`}>{NATIONAL.elecCents.toFixed(1)}¢/kWh</td>
                <td data-label={`Natural gas, ${PRICE_PERIODS.gasYear} average`}>{usd(NATIONAL.gasPerTherm)}/therm</td>
                <td data-label="Heating oil">{usd(NATIONAL.oilPerGal)}/gal</td>
                <td data-label="Propane">{usd(NATIONAL.propanePerGal)}/gal</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small data-note">
          Electricity: EIA Electric Power Monthly, residential average for {PRICE_PERIODS.elecMonth} (released {PRICE_PERIODS.elecReleased}).
          Natural gas: EIA residential price, {PRICE_PERIODS.gasYear} annual average, which is steadier than a summer month; EIA calls it inclusive of taxes and delivery charges.
          Heating oil and propane: EIA weekly residential prices for the week of {PRICE_PERIODS.oilWeek}, excluding taxes; EIA publishes them for some states only, and the planner leaves those fuels out where there’s no local price unless your home uses them.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="card data-details">
        <summary><strong>Climate and air conditioning in the 26 metro areas</strong> <span class="small">(NOAA 1991–2020 normals, EIA RECS 2020)</span></summary>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table class="stack">
            <thead><tr><th>Metro area</th><th>Heating degree days</th><th>Cooling degree days</th><th>IECC zone</th><th>AC, typical home</th><th>Station</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {metroRows.map(({ m, ac }) => (
                <tr>
                  <td data-label="Metro area"><a href={`/us/cities/${m.slug}/`}>{m.data.name}</a></td>
                  <td data-label="Heating degree days">{int(m.climate?.hdd65 ?? 0)}</td>
                  <td data-label="Cooling degree days">{int(m.climate?.cdd65 ?? 0)}</td>
                  <td data-label="IECC zone">{m.climate?.iecc2021ClimateZone ?? '—'}</td>
                  <td data-label="AC, typical home">about {int(Math.round(ac / 100) * 100)} kWh a year</td>
                  <td data-label="Station" class="mono">{m.climate?.station?.name ?? '—'}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small data-note">Degree days are base 65&nbsp;°F. Metros are listed coldest first. “AC, typical home” is the model’s yearly air conditioning for a 1,600 sq ft detached house built 1981–1995 with an average central AC.</p>
      </details>

      <h3 class="src-h">Sources</h3>
      <ul class="small src-list">
        {noaa && <li><a href={noaa.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">NOAA NCEI U.S. Climate Normals 1991–2020</a>: heating and cooling degree days</li>}
        <li><a href={POPULATION_SOURCE.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{POPULATION_SOURCE.label}</a>: the 26 metro areas</li>
        <li><a href={P.electricity.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">EIA Electric Power Monthly, Table 5.6.A</a> · <a href={P.naturalGas.annualUrl} rel="noopener" target="_blank">EIA residential natural gas prices (annual)</a> · <a href={P.naturalGas.heatContent?.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">EIA heat content of natural gas</a> · <a href={P.heatingOil.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">EIA Heating Oil and Propane Update</a></li>
        {estarSrc && <li><a href={estarSrc.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{estarSrc.label}</a>: attic and floor targets by climate zone</li>}
        {coolSources.map((s) => <li><a href={s.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{s.label}</a></li>)}
        {zipSources.map((s) => <li><a href={s.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{s.label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
      </details>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script type="application/json" id="planner-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(payload).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')} />
  <GlossaryRuntime country="us" />
</Base>

